  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how chemical release from single vesicles in a cell can be measured electrochemically and how different exocytosis modes change what is released. On the left, a stimulation pipette triggers secretion from a cell packed with vesicles. A nearby disc microelectrode detects oxidizable transmitter molecules as they exit the cell, converting them into an electrical current spike. The small current-versus-time trace suggests that each release event appears as a brief amperometric peak, reflecting the amount and timing of molecules escaping from a vesicle.\n\nThe right side compares three visible release behaviors. In “kiss and run,” a vesicle briefly contacts the membrane, opens a small fusion pore, releases only a little content, and then is retrieved intact. In “partial release” or “open and closed,” the vesicle opens more but still does not empty completely before closing again. In “full release,” fusion appears to progress further so the vesicle empties much more completely, leaving a larger cloud of released molecules outside the cell. Overall, the image emphasizes that secretion is not always all-or-none: vesicles can release small, partial, or nearly complete amounts, and these differences can be captured by microelectrode measurements.",
